mits every relation type. Umbra's field needs CALLS,\nUSES_TYPE, DATA_FLOWS, TESTS and FILE_CHANGES_WITH. Run\n  entire graph capabilities --json\nand check which relations this build actually emits by default. If the default is\nnot full, find the flag that makes Umbra's snapshot and impact calls request it,\nand note it. If the relations Umbra needs are unavailable, that is a degradation,\nnot an error: apply the degradation table and say so in the header. Do this\nbefore the demo run, because thin relations mean wrong demo numbers.\n\nSTEP 4, first commit: the four planning documents plus HANDOFF.md and\nKICKOFF_PROMPT.md only, no product code. Create the \"initial understanding and\nintended architecture\" checkpoint against it. The checkpoint must carry real\nreasoning: the four states, why the subtraction is the product, the settled\ndecisions, and the rejected options from NOTES.md.\n\nSTEP 5, second commit: the product code as one clearly labelled import commit,\nwith NOTES.md and the corrections list. Do not fabricate a phase-by-phase history\nthat did not happen in this repository. Then verify the graft:\n  go test ./...\n  entire umbra HEAD --test \"pytest -v\" --out ./out\nagainst the fixture app. The claim to match exactly: 8 probes selected, 5 cracked,\nfull sweep, 0 leaks. If the numbers differ in this clone, find out why before\n11:00 and write the reason down. Never use pytest -q; it degrades verify to\nexit-code-only.\n\nSTEP 6, graph evidence, recorded as you go, worth 15 points:\n  one entire graph search or def lookup\n  one entire graph impact analysis run before a change you then make\n  entire graph diff --base <first commit> --head HEAD as the final semantic diff\nWrite each into BUILDATHON.md with what it found and how you verified it against\nsource and tests. Graph output is evidence, not an oracle; never present an\nuncertain finding as fact.\n\nSTEP 7, BUILDATHON.md in the repository root, using the participant guide's exact\noutline: name, one-sentence summary, problem and intended user, selected track and\nwhy Entire is essential, architecture and main workflow, Graph findings and\nverification, Noon Curveball, checkpoint links and what each proves, setup and\nrun and test instructions, known limitations and next steps. Fill everything now\nexcept the Curveball section. Limitations must include: the examined set comes\nfrom tool activity so shell-driven agents leave little trace; the stale-worktree\nfailure mode and where to delete; that checks are only trusted once observed\nfailing; and that umbra clean is documented but not built.\n\nSTEP 8, Runnable state committed, tests green,\npre-noon checkpoint created recording current intent, architecture, completed\nwork, unresolved work and technical risks.\n\nSTEP 10, final 20 minutes final commit pushed and the SHA recorded, project\nlaunches from a clean checkout via the documented path, four checkpoints open and\nexplain their milestones, semantic diff recorded, BUILDATHON.md complete and free\nof secrets, tests covering the critical and Curveball behaviour green, fallback\nscreenshots available locally.\n\nmirror and plugin working yes or no, which relations the\ncapabilities call reports, the demo numbers observed, which graph evidence is\nrecorded, and which BUILDATHON.md sections are still empty."},"uuid":"f6c9afb6-4349-4c2d-8280-762bc220b7fb","timestamp":"2026-09-06T04:15:07.587Z","permissionMode":"auto","origin":{"kind":"human"},"promptSource":"typed","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/utkuputku/Desktop/Umbra","sessionId":"8017734d-cdf6-4a18-b2f8-f4b5f637ec35","version":"2.1.261","gitBranch":"main","slug":"twinkling-waddling-dragon"}
