{"parentUuid":"bfe82356-208d-4695-a759-763e81efdb1f","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"queued_command","prompt":"<task-notification>\n<task-id>bswivwuil</task-id>\n<tool-use-id>toolu_01BPZve9BVrVStcsnJNrzRr1</tool-use-id>\n<output-file>/tmp/claude-1000/-home-utkuputku-Desktop-Umbra/abbf9c0c-4f5e-46cd-a7be-70d09966a014/tasks/bswivwuil.output</output-file>\n<status>completed</status>\n<summary>Background command \"Run the semantic diff evidence\" completed (exit code 0)</summary>\n</task-notification>","commandMode":"task-notification","timestamp":"2026-09-06T05:04:44.871Z"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"ac485fb7-5413-420a-a774-baaf4274ff4f","timestamp":"2026-09-06T05:04:44.871Z","session_id":"abbf9c0c-4f5e-46cd-a7be-70d09966a014","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/utkuputku/Desktop/Umbra","sessionId":"8017734d-cdf6-4a18-b2f8-f4b5f637ec35","version":"2.1.261","gitBranch":"main","slug":"twinkling-waddling-dragon"}
{"parentUuid":"ac485fb7-5413-420a-a774-baaf4274ff4f","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"edited_text_file","filename":"/home/utkuputku/entire-graph/BUILDATHON.md","snippet":"1\t# Umbra\n2\t\n3\t## One sentence\n4\t\n5\tUmbra finds the code an AI agent's change affects but the agent never looked\n6\tat, by subtracting the session's attention from the change's blast radius.\n7\t\n8\t## Problem and intended user\n9\t\n10\tThe reviewer of an agent-authored change, who has to decide where to look\n11\tfirst.\n12\t\n13\tA clean diff and a confident commit message tell that reviewer what changed.\n14\tThey do not tell the reviewer what the agent never opened. The everyday case is\n15\ta signature change with seven callers, three of them tests in a file the\n16\tsession never touched, committed with a message saying the callers were checked\n17\tbecause one test file happened to run and pass.\n18\t\n19\tUmbra answers a question no existing tool answers: of everything this change\n20\tcan reach, what did the session never look at, and which tests reach that\n21\tshadow.\n22\t\n23\t## Track, and why Entire is essential\n24\t\n25\t**Track E2, Graph Intelligence.**\n26\t\n27\tThe product is a subtraction, and neither half is available anywhere else.\n28\t\n29\t- **Entire Graph** supplies the blast radius: every dependent of every changed\n30\t  symbol, with relation types, call site lines and dependent counts. Without it\n31\t  there is no field to subtract from, and reimplementing it would mean writing\n32\t  a multi-language semantic indexer.\n33\t- **Entire Checkpoints** supply the attention: the session's tool activity,\n34\t  what was read, with which line ranges, in what order, and what the agent said\n35\t  about it. Without it there is no attention to subtract, and no other record\n36\t  of an agent's session exists at this fidelity.\n37\t\n38\tEither half alone is a known thing. Impact analysis on its own is Graph's\n39\texisting feature. A list of files an agent read is a log. The difference\n40\tbetween them is the finding, and it is only computable because one system\n41\tholds both.\n42\t\n43\tUmbra lives in `umbra/` as its own Go module and imports no package from\n44\t`entire-graph`. It talks to Graph the way any agent does, through the\n45\tdocumented CLI surface.\n46\t\n47\t## Architecture and main workflow\n48\t\n49\tOne static binary, `entire-umbra`, dispatched by the Entire CLI kubectl style\n50\tas `entire umbra <checkpoint>`. There is no model anywhere in it; the core is\n51\tdeterministic and offline.\n52\t\n53\t```\n54\tentire umbra <ref> --test \"pytest -v\" --out ./out\n55\t```\n56\t\n57\tSeven steps:\n58\t\n59\t1. **Resolve.** Turn a checkpoint id or commit-ish into a checkpoint, a commit\n60\t   and its parent, and record how the pairing was made.\n61\t2. **Check out.** Detached worktrees of head and parent under the plugin data\n62\t   directory, never the user's working tree.\n63\t3. **Load the field.** `graph snapshot` for symbols and relations,\n64\t   `graph capabilities` for which relations this build emits, and\n65\t   `graph commit` for the changed symbols and their change kinds.\n66\t4. **Walk it.** Breadth first to the requested depth, keeping each dependent's\n67\t   shortest path back to a source.\n68\t5. **Subtract.** Build the examined set from the checkpoint's tool activity and\n69\t   the agent's own words, then classify every node as lit, penumbra, umbra or\n70\t   unknown.\n71\t6. **Probe.** Select the tests that reach shadow, record a baseline on the\n72\t   parent with `graph verify`, and adjudicate the same command on head, so a\n73\t   test that was already red is not reported as a new failure.\n74\t7. **Sweep.** Run the whole suite once. Any test that changed state but was\n75\t   never selected is a leak, and carries the reason selection missed it.\n76\t\n77\tOutput is a terminal table, a JSON report, a one page packet for a pull request\n78\tcomment, and a self contained HTML eclipse map that replays the session's\n79\tattention in order. Exit code 2 on a `--fail-on` condition, for CI.\n80\t\n81\t### The four states\n82\t\n83\t| State | Meaning |\n84\t|---|---|\n85\t| lit | read with a range covering the symbol, at or after the change began, or edited |\n86\t| penumbra | half seen, in five tiers: glance, glimpse, quoted, afterimage, echo |\n87\t| umbra | nothing in the session touched the file or named it |\n88\t| unknown | the transcript carries no tool events and no mentions |\n89\t\n90\tMissing data is a state, never an error.\n91\t\n92\t## Graph findings and verification\n93\t\n94\tGraph output is evidence, not an oracle. Each finding below says how it was\n95\tchecked against source and tests.\n96\t\n97\t### Finding 1: the relations Umbra needs are present, verified twice\n98\t\n99\t`entire graph capabilities --json` reports three indexing profiles.\n100\t`syntax-only` emits DEFINES and CONTAINS. `fast` adds CALLS and boundaries.\n101\t`full` emits all thirty relation types.\n102\t\n103\tUmbra's field needs CALLS, USES_TYPE, DATA_FLOWS, TESTS and FILE_CHANGES_WITH.\n104\tUnder `fast`, only CALLS is available, which would silently thin the field and\n105\tproduce wrong numbers.\n106\t\n107\t**Verified.** The default profile on both `graph snapshot` and `graph impact`\n108\tis `full`, read from `--help`, and Umbra passes no `--profile`, checked by\n109\tgrep across the source. So it already gets `full`. That is the reading of the\n110\tdocumentation; the measurement is better. A real snapshot of this repository\n111\temits, repo-wide and within the fixture app:\n112\t\n113\t| Relation | Repo | Fixture app |\n114\t|---|---|---|\n115\t| CALLS | 1856 | 29 |\n116\t| DATA_FLOWS | 441 | 11 |\n117\t| USES_TYPE | 281 | 9 |\n118\t| FILE_CHANGES_WITH | 162 | 1 |\n119\t| TESTS | 11 | 2 |\n120\t\n121\tAll five present. No degradation applies and no flag is needed.\n122\t\n123\t**One thing that looked like a problem and was not.** Python's\n124\t`relation_support_by_language` entry omits TESTS and FILE_CHANGES_WITH. Chasing\n125\tit rather than assuming: those two are listed for **0 of 185 languages**,\n126\tbecause they are derived rather than parsed, TESTS heuristically and\n127\tFILE_CHANGES_WITH from git co-change history. Their absence from a per-language\n128\tlist is expected. The snapshot count above is what settles it.\n129\t\n130\tWorth noting for anyone reusing this: `graph search` defaults to `--profile\n131\tfast`, not `full`, unlike snapshot and impact. Umbra does not use search, so\n132\tits field is unaffected.\n133\t\n134\t### Finding 2: search located the word, not the decision\n135\t\n136\tQuery: *where does Umbra decide a node is umbra rather than penumbra*.\n137\t\n138\t`entire graph search --profile full` returned **LOW CONFIDENCE**, reporting\n139\tthat ranks 1 and 2 were tied 0.0100 apart and that the ranking did not choose.\n140\tIts top hit was `appendPenumbra` at `umbra/internal/report/assets/umbra.js:382`,\n141\twhich **draws** a penumbra disc and decides nothing.\n142\t\n143\t**Verified against source.** The decision is `ClassifyAt` at\n144\t`umbra/internal/shadow/classify.go:35`, confirmed with `entire graph def\n145\t--symbol ClassifyAt`, and pinned by the cross-implementation test at\n146\t`umbra/internal/shadow/jsmirror_test.go:122`, which feeds recorded scenarios\n147\tthrough the Go classifier and the browser reimplementation and compares every\n148\tstate and tier.\n149\t\n150\t**What this is evidence of.** The tool was honest about its own uncertainty and\n151\tthe warning was correct. Taking the top hit without reading it would have sent\n152\tan editor to the renderer instead of the classifier. This is the reason the\n153\tfinding is written up with its verification rather than as a fact.\n154\t\n155\t### Finding 3: the demo numbers, and why they differ in this clone\n156\t\n157\tThe claim from the source repository is exact and reproducible **there**: 8\n158\tprobes selected, 5 cracked, full sweep, 0 leaks, naming both refund tests and\n159\tthe three service tests. That was re-run today against the freshly built\n160\tplugin and still holds.\n161\t\n162\t**It does not reproduce in this fork, and the reason is not a defect.**\n163\t\n164\tThe claim is a property of commit `0063443`, the seeded signature change to\n165\t`compute_total` in the fixture app. That commit belongs to the source\n166\trepository's history, which was deliberately not carried across. Four\n167\tend-to-end tests say so directly rather than failing quietly:\n168\t\n169\t```\n170\t\"0063443\" is neither a checkpoint nor a commit:\n171\tgit rev-parse 0063443 exited 128: fatal: Needed a single revision\n\n... [130 lines truncated] ..."},"type":"attachment","uuid":"5181c308-d97e-4f7d-9f9d-88555059f239","timestamp":"2026-09-06T05:04:51.842Z","session_id":"abbf9c0c-4f5e-46cd-a7be-70d09966a014","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/utkuputku/Desktop/Umbra","sessionId":"8017734d-cdf6-4a18-b2f8-f4b5f637ec35","version":"2.1.261","gitBranch":"main","slug":"twinkling-waddling-dragon"}
