{"parentUuid":"16334ed5-253c-494a-a946-fe68a5982bce","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CemoUwmEwhEb2kpxCK5hV","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01LKQ8DYR2mdRrPzQLeCzk9p","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /home/utkuputku/entire-graph\nfor c in e41af1db 7744e34a 26ba3d70 4742e272; do echo \"=== $c ===\"; git log -1 --format='%s%n%b' $c | head -18; echo; done","description":"Read the four curveball implementation commits"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":901,"cache_read_input_tokens":943524,"output_tokens":160,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":901,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":3,"output_tokens":160,"cache_read_input_tokens":943524,"cache_creation_input_tokens":901,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":901},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null},"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011CemoUnj26eBXEsxnBAxPi","type":"assistant","uuid":"51752466-0636-4aec-b378-47fd4495474c","timestamp":"2026-09-06T07:36:43.034Z","effort":"high","session_id":"abbf9c0c-4f5e-46cd-a7be-70d09966a014","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/utkuputku/Desktop/Umbra","sessionId":"8017734d-cdf6-4a18-b2f8-f4b5f637ec35","version":"2.1.261","gitBranch":"main","slug":"twinkling-waddling-dragon"}
{"parentUuid":"51752466-0636-4aec-b378-47fd4495474c","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"87aba2de-de35-4ff1-9e3a-cbed7d26b95f","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LKQ8DYR2mdRrPzQLeCzk9p","type":"tool_result","content":"=== e41af1db ===\numbra: keep the resolution and confidence Graph puts on every relation\nUpstream tags every relation record with a resolution and a confidence, and\ncapabilities reports which relation types are heuristic per language. Umbra\nread the relation type and discarded both, so a name-only guess and a call the\nprovider resolved to a definition reached the map as the same edge.\n\nThe snapshot loader now keeps resolution, confidence and the per-relation\nwarning codes, reads the header line and the trailing summary record for what\nthe snapshot says about its own completeness, and counts malformed and\nunusable records instead of skipping them silently. An empty field built from\nnothing readable used to look exactly like a clean one.\n\nThe walk carries the weakest hop along each path rather than only the first\nhop's relation, because a chain is only as resolved as its least resolved\nlink, and names the hop that made a path heuristic. Resolution enters the\npath tie-break below depth and family, so it can change which path is quoted\nfor a node but never which nodes are in the field.\n\n\n=== 7744e34a ===\numbra: three tiers on every dependent, in all four surfaces\nconfirmed, heuristic and needs verification, on every dependent in the\nterminal table, the JSON, the HTML map and the packet.\n\nThis is a fourth axis beside state, tier and score, not a fifth state. The\nfour states say what the SESSION saw; this says what the GRAPH knows. The four\nstates are untouched, and the six ranking factors are untouched: Score reads\nnone of these fields.\n\nconfirmed          every hop the provider resolved to a definition\nheuristic          the relation attaching this node is one the provider\n                   derives rather than parses, which on this build is CALLS\n                   resolved as anything but exact or import_resolved, plus\n                   HANDLES_ROUTE, HANDLES_TOOL and the other five capabilities\n                   names, plus co-change, plus every dependent count\nneeds verification reached through a heuristic link the reader cannot see, or\n                   emitted under a partial failure or a degraded snapshot\n\n\n=== 26ba3d70 ===\numbra: report the partial analysis Graph already tells us about\nThe snapshot header and its trailing summary carry warnings, partial_failures,\ncompleteness stats and a completeness level. graph impact prints the same\nblock above every answer plus a per-query completeness_scope. Umbra read past\nall of it, so a report built from a degraded snapshot claimed a clean reading\nof the repository on evidence that never said so.\n\nWhen any of it is present the header now says the field may be incomplete and\nnames the reason in the graph's own codes: the completeness level with the\nparsed-file count, the partial failures by code, the warnings by code, the\nrecords the loader could not read, and any impact answer the graph flagged for\na changed symbol. The degraded Python completeness captured this morning,\nW_DATA_FLOW_EVIDENCE_UNMERGED, is one of the cases with a test.\n\nA malformed snapshot record is counted and reported rather than dropped. A\nsilently empty field currently looks like a clean one, which is the same shape\nas the false zero the external review found in the sweep.\n\n\n=== 4742e272 ===\numbra: a fixture that represents incomplete analysis, and the ninth scenario\nfixtures/partial is a small Python project built to contain analysis the graph\ncannot complete, and fixtures/recorded/dynamic-dispatch is the scenario driven\nfrom it. Its graph side is a separate capture taken from a throwaway\nrepository holding only that directory, so the summary record describes the\nfixture and nothing else. Every number asserted was measured from real\nprovider output before it was written down.\n\nThree things at once. dispatch.py reaches the changed symbol three ways at\nruntime, through a handler table, through getattr and through importlib, and\nthe provider draws not one edge for any of them; the four tests that exercise\nthose paths are absent from the field entirely, which is correct and is why\nthe report has to say the field is a lower bound. billing.py gives a chain\nwhose three tiers fall out of real resolutions with nothing constructed in the\ntest: apply import_resolved confirmed, quote type_inferred heuristic, invoice\nexact hop through an inferred chain needs verification, and the test that\nreaches it needs verification too. legacy_rates.cob is inventory-only and\nrate_table.min.json is over the minified threshold, so partial_failures is\nShell cwd was reset to /home/utkuputku/Desktop/Umbra","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"e0ed4d1c-7245-46a6-9392-4da56b08bda3","timestamp":"2026-09-06T07:36:45.003Z","toolUseResult":{"stdout":"=== e41af1db ===\numbra: keep the resolution and confidence Graph puts on every relation\nUpstream tags every relation record with a resolution and a confidence, and\ncapabilities reports which relation types are heuristic per language. Umbra\nread the relation type and discarded both, so a name-only guess and a call the\nprovider resolved to a definition reached the map as the same edge.\n\nThe snapshot loader now keeps resolution, confidence and the per-relation\nwarning codes, reads the header line and the trailing summary record for what\nthe snapshot says about its own completeness, and counts malformed and\nunusable records instead of skipping them silently. An empty field built from\nnothing readable used to look exactly like a clean one.\n\nThe walk carries the weakest hop along each path rather than only the first\nhop's relation, because a chain is only as resolved as its least resolved\nlink, and names the hop that made a path heuristic. Resolution enters the\npath tie-break below depth and family, so it can change which path is quoted\nfor a node but never which nodes are in the field.\n\n\n=== 7744e34a ===\numbra: three tiers on every dependent, in all four surfaces\nconfirmed, heuristic and needs verification, on every dependent in the\nterminal table, the JSON, the HTML map and the packet.\n\nThis is a fourth axis beside state, tier and score, not a fifth state. The\nfour states say what the SESSION saw; this says what the GRAPH knows. The four\nstates are untouched, and the six ranking factors are untouched: Score reads\nnone of these fields.\n\nconfirmed          every hop the provider resolved to a definition\nheuristic          the relation attaching this node is one the provider\n                   derives rather than parses, which on this build is CALLS\n                   resolved as anything but exact or import_resolved, plus\n                   HANDLES_ROUTE, HANDLES_TOOL and the other five capabilities\n                   names, plus co-change, plus every dependent count\nneeds verification reached through a heuristic link the reader cannot see, or\n                   emitted under a partial failure or a degraded snapshot\n\n\n=== 26ba3d70 ===\numbra: report the partial analysis Graph already tells us about\nThe snapshot header and its trailing summary carry warnings, partial_failures,\ncompleteness stats and a completeness level. graph impact prints the same\nblock above every answer plus a per-query completeness_scope. Umbra read past\nall of it, so a report built from a degraded snapshot claimed a clean reading\nof the repository on evidence that never said so.\n\nWhen any of it is present the header now says the field may be incomplete and\nnames the reason in the graph's own codes: the completeness level with the\nparsed-file count, the partial failures by code, the warnings by code, the\nrecords the loader could not read, and any impact answer the graph flagged for\na changed symbol. The degraded Python completeness captured this morning,\nW_DATA_FLOW_EVIDENCE_UNMERGED, is one of the cases with a test.\n\nA malformed snapshot record is counted and reported rather than dropped. A\nsilently empty field currently looks like a clean one, which is the same shape\nas the false zero the external review found in the sweep.\n\n\n=== 4742e272 ===\numbra: a fixture that represents incomplete analysis, and the ninth scenario\nfixtures/partial is a small Python project built to contain analysis the graph\ncannot complete, and fixtures/recorded/dynamic-dispatch is the scenario driven\nfrom it. Its graph side is a separate capture taken from a throwaway\nrepository holding only that directory, so the summary record describes the\nfixture and nothing else. Every number asserted was measured from real\nprovider output before it was written down.\n\nThree things at once. dispatch.py reaches the changed symbol three ways at\nruntime, through a handler table, through getattr and through importlib, and\nthe provider draws not one edge for any of them; the four tests that exercise\nthose paths are absent from the field entirely, which is correct and is why\nthe report has to say the field is a lower bound. billing.py gives a chain\nwhose three tiers fall out of real resolutions with nothing constructed in the\ntest: apply import_resolved confirmed, quote type_inferred heuristic, invoice\nexact hop through an inferred chain needs verification, and the test that\nreaches it needs verification too. legacy_rates.cob is inventory-only and\nrate_table.min.json is over the minified threshold, so partial_failures is","stderr":"\nShell cwd was reset to /home/utkuputku/Desktop/Umbra","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"51752466-0636-4aec-b378-47fd4495474c","session_id":"abbf9c0c-4f5e-46cd-a7be-70d09966a014","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/home/utkuputku/Desktop/Umbra","sessionId":"8017734d-cdf6-4a